    "lawCode": "Amd §§380.60 & 380.70, add §380.65, CP L; amd §601, Cor L",
    "lawSection": "Criminal Procedure Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S5542\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act\nto amend the criminal procedure law and the correction law, in relation\nto the delivery of sentence and commitment documents\n\nPURPOSE OF BILL:\n\nThis bill would clarify that a sentence and commitment or certificate\nof conviction, and any order of protection, must be delivered\ntogether with a defendant who receives a sentence of imprisonment, at\nthe time of his or her delivery to a state or local correctional\ninstitution, or a facility operated by the Office of Children and\nFamily Services.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nThis bill would make a technical amendment to Criminal Procedure Law\n380.60 of the Criminal Procedure Law (CPL), add a new Section 380.65\nto the CPL, and make conforming amendments to Section 380.70 of the\nCPL and Section 601 of the Correction Law (CL).\n\nEXISTING LAW:\n\nCurrently, CPL § 380.60 states that in all cases other than when a\nsentence of death is pronounced, a \"certificate of conviction\"\nshowing the sentence pronounced by the court, or a certified copy\nthereof, constitutes the authority for execution of sentence and\nserves as the order of commitment. In addition, CPL § 380.70, which\ndeals with sentencing minutes, provides that a certificate of\nconviction and any order of protection must be mailed to the\ninstitution to which the defendant is delivered within thirty days\nfrom the date sentence was imposed. CL § 601 provides that whenever\nan inmate is delivered to a state facility, the transporting officer\nmust also deliver a certified copy of the sentence, a certificate of\nconviction and a copy of any order or temporary order of protection.\n\nSTATEMENT IN SUPPORT:\n\nCriminal Procedure Law § 380.60, pertaining to the authority for the\nexecution of sentence, currently states that a \"certificate of\nconviction\" or a certified copy thereof is the legal document that\nconfers the authority for the execution of sentence and serves as\nthe order of commitment. In actuality, the technical title of the\ndocument that confers authority to execute the sentence, and that is\nroutinely delivered to the Department together with the individual\nsentenced to state imprisonment, is a \"sentence and commitment.\" As\nsuch, it is appropriate that this section of law be amended to refer\nto the correctly titled document.\n\nCriminal Procedure Law § 380.70 accurately sets forth that in any case\nwhere a person receives either a determinate or indeterminate\nsentence of imprisonment, the sentencing minutes must be transcribed\n\n\nand separately sent to the institution to which the defendant has\nbeen delivered within thirty days of the pronouncement of sentence.\n\nAs part of the Sentencing Reform Act of 1995 (SRA), it was determined\nthat the commitment document should contain greater specificity with\nregard to the crime for which an individual is being committed.\nSpecifically, it was determined that the commitment should also\nindicate, to the extent applicable, the subdivision, paragraph and\nsubparagraph of the penal law or other statute under which the\ndefendant was convicted. Due to an oversight, however, when the\nLegislature adopted the SRA in 1995, this provision of law was\nmistakenly inserted into the same section of law that deals with\nsentencing minutes, thereby creating the mis-impression that a\ncommitment, like sentencing minutes, is delivered to the Department\nseparately from the convicted individual. This error was continued\nwhen the law was subsequently expanded to encompass orders of\nprotection.\n\nIn actuality, the commitment document (\"sentence and commitment\") must\nbe included within the packet of documentation delivered together\nwith the individual being committed to state prison. See CL § 601.\nCurrently, CPL § 390.60 expressly requires that pre-sentence reports\nand related information must be delivered together with the\nindividual being committed. However, the CPL contains no comparable\nrequirement that the sentence of commitment and any order of\nprotection be delivered together with such individual. For\nclarification purposes, it is appropriate that a comparable provision\nof law be created to separately cover the delivery of a sentence and\ncommitment or certificate of conviction, and any order of protection,\ntogether with the defendant. The timely receipt of an order of\nprotection is particularly important in the case of domestic violence\nvictims.\n\nLastly, for purposes of uniformity and consistency, it is appropriate\nthat CL § 601 be amended to indicate that the official document being\ndelivered together with a defendant is a \"sentence and commitment\"\nor certificate of conviction, or certified copy thereof.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis bill was delivered to the Legislature in 2010 and introduced as\nS.5517/A.7951.  It did not advance beyond the Codes Committee in\neither House.\n\nBUDGETARY IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nThis bill would take effect on September 1, 2011; with\nprovisions.\n\n",
